  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>A rather small (around 20 cm) spiny eel with a very contrasting pattern. It was described scientifically in 1893 but is still new for the hobby.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species is endemic to the Lake Inle region in Burma.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lake Inle has medium hard water with a slightly alkaline pH. Many pretty and very suitable aquarium fish have already been imported from Lake Inle.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>When keeping spiny eels it is necessary to offer a number of hiding places as well as fine sand, which enables the fish to bury themselves at least in some parts of the tank. Plants are ignored. The worst that can happen is that a spiny eel tries to bury itself under a plant.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Mastacembelids are exclusively carnivorous. Spiny eels are fed with all the usual live and frozen food, and most of them also accept granulated food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Spiny eels are generally very peaceful to conspecifics and to other fish. Very small fish, however, are eaten. Due to the quite small mouth of this spiny eel, other fish from 4–5 cm upwards are usually safe.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>There are two phenotypes in the imported specimens. Some fish are larger, heavier built and have a very dark coloration, others are smaller, more elongate and rather brownish. This possibly represents sexual dimorphism.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is generally possible to breed spiny eels in the aquarium. They prefer to spawn in the roots of floating plants near the water surface.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/22-spiny-eels/mastacembelus_caudiocellatus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Mastacembelus: ancient Greek, means \"the one that throws oneself a bite\"; probably it was thought that these fish can use their snout like the trunk of an elephant, which is wrong. caudiocellatus: Latin, means \"with an eyespot in the caudal\".</p>"
    }
  ],
